Package: mica-bluetooth
Version: 5.82-mica3
Source-Date-Epoch: 1793232000
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: Mica OS <hi@micaos.dev>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: bluez, rfkill
Description: Mica OS Bluetooth support
 The generic HCI-attach program /usr/lib/mica/hwinit-bt and the bind of
 /var/lib/bluetooth from the STATE partition, for either init.
 .
 Under systemd: mica-bt.service, enabled by the multi-user.target.wants link
 this package owns and inert without /etc/mica/bt.conf
 (ConditionPathExists=), and var-lib-bluetooth.mount with its
 local-fs.target.wants link. Under OpenRC: /etc/init.d/mica-bt, which starts
 nothing without /etc/mica/bt.conf, and /etc/init.d/mica-bluetoothd, which
 binds the pairing keys and runs bluetoothd under supervise-daemon, in the
 default runlevel as bluetooth.service is enabled under systemd; mica-bt is in
 no runlevel -- the product links it.
 .
 bluetoothd reads /usr/lib/mica/bluetooth/main.conf through -f under both
 inits (a drop-in for bluez's bluetooth.service, and mica-bluetoothd), in place
 of bluez's conffile /etc/bluetooth/main.conf: an LE connection interval of
 11.25-15 ms, everything else bluetoothd's default.
 .
 The UART, the host-controller protocol and the link speed hwinit-bt reads
 from /etc/mica/bt.conf, the radio kernel modules and the firmware are board
 facts and live in the board's package. The bind keeps pairings on STATE:
 bluez writes link keys under /var/lib/bluetooth, /var is disposable, and a
 pairing is a credential. rfkill is required because the radio is
 soft-blocked out of the box on the boards Mica OS targets.
 .
 Wi-Fi is mica-wifi and mica-wifi-ap; this package pulls bluez alone.
